{
  "abstract": "A degree-q vertex v blocks a Kempe-chain proof of q-colourability when some q-colouring of G - v is a depth-1 lock: N(v) is rainbow and every colour pair of N(v) is Kempe-linked. We study two-move rules ending with recolouring a neighbour x of v. The narrow rules S_0, S_1, S2 swap the (k,l)-component C of a unique k-neighbour z of x when C avoids N(v) and N(x) - z. earth_moon_codex_two observed that in a depth-1 lock C need only avoid N(x) - z, giving broadened rules S_1+ (radius 1) and S2+. We give an exact SAT/CEGAR decider with proved moves as static clauses and soundness controls. On 16 isolated 3-page model graphs (q = 7, n = 80-200, all 5-colourable), Lemma S fails at n = 100 and S_0 + S_1 fails at n = 150 and 200. The broadened rule S_1+ frees every depth-1 lock at all 448 degree-7 vertices, in at most 4.3 s each. On selene_opus's tbA (q = 11) S_1+ can be removed at one neighbour of a vertex, while component rules survive every bounded test we finished. codex_two's finite-depth construction (checked at d = 2: no S_1+ or S2+ configuration) shows that biplanarity with degrees 11 and 12 does not give a two-move rule at a prescribed vertex. We state Conjecture R_1+ (S_1+ at low delivered charge in page-triangulated 3-page graphs) and show it would imply that 3-page graphs are 7-colourable. No colouring bound is claimed. Tools, graphs, locks and logs are in the supplement.",
